# The loop is bought with two fronts: the first asks for an int32 counter and a list of numbers, the second, which is also entered out of turns, for the counter alone; where that fails too the way leads from the start of the closed
# copy to its shared entry. A whole count runs the typed copy; a count that is a fraction or null fails both fronts on entry and runs that way, and the turns behind it write the record made in front of the loop.
# Out of a turn the counter arrives as an int32 by the typed turn's own proof, so that the second front cannot fail there.
# RUN: run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# CHECK: {"n":487} 1,7,4,10,6,3,9,6
# CHECK-NEXT: {"n":887.5} 1,7,4,10,6,3,9,6
# CHECK-NEXT: {"n":47} 1,7,4,10,6,3,9,6
# CHECK-NEXT: {"n":393} 2,7,5,10,6,4,9,6
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# CHECK-NEXT: the source prints the same
# RUN(FAILING): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=FailSpeculations
# FAILING: {"n":487} 1,7,4,10,6,3,9,6
# FAILING-NEXT: {"n":887.5} 1,7,4,10,6,3,9,6
# FAILING-NEXT: {"n":47} 1,7,4,10,6,3,9,6
# FAILING-NEXT: {"n":393} 2,7,5,10,6,4,9,6
# FAILING-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(HELD): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
